  workflows                                small/mid businesses unserved.

  Low price + Generic    Buildertrend,     Accessible but shallow. Doesn\'t
  workflows              Fieldwire         understand trades. Works for
                                           simple residential only.

  High price + Trade     None significant  Procore has some depth but it\'s
  depth                                    generic, expensive, and
                                           sub-collaboration is absent.

  **Low price + Trade    **\[PLATFORM      This is the unoccupied market.
  depth = WHITE SPACE**  NAME\]**          3.7M US construction businesses
                                           that are underserved by every
                                           current player.
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------

**4.2 The Cross-Tenant Moat**

Cross-tenant collaboration is not just a feature --- it is an
architectural moat. No competitor can replicate it without rebuilding
their data architecture from scratch. Here is why:

-   Procore is single-tenant architecture at the project level. GCs
    \'invite\' subs to their Procore project as users --- subs have no
    independent environment, no independent financials, no data
    sovereignty. They are guests in the GC\'s account.

-   Buildertrend has no sub collaboration --- subs are vendors in the
    GC\'s account, period.

-   eSUB serves subs --- but has no GC collaboration capability
    whatsoever.

-   No platform has a shared project layer where two independent tenants
    operate their own full environments and selectively share governed
    data. This requires multi-tenant architecture designed for it from
    day one.

> **The Moat:**
>
> Replicating cross-tenant collaboration for a competitor would require:
> (1) rebuilding their data model from single-tenant to true
> multi-tenant, (2) redesigning their authentication and permission
> model, (3) building the shared event bridge, and (4) rebuilding their
> product for dual-sided use. This is a 12--18 month engineering effort
> for an established platform --- not a feature sprint.

**4.3 The Network Effect Flywheel**

Cross-tenant collaboration creates the platform\'s most powerful growth
mechanic:

-   1 GC tenant signs up → invites 5 subs to portal (free) → 1--2 subs
    convert to paid tenants

-   Each new sub tenant brings their own sub-tier vendors → another wave
    of invites

-   GCs prefer the platform because their subs are already on it →
    creates GC acquisition from the sub side

-   As tenant count grows, the probability that any new company\'s GC or
    sub is already on platform increases

-   Network effects compound --- each tenant makes the platform more
    valuable for every other tenant

This flywheel does not exist for any competitor. Procore\'s \'invite
subs\' model creates dependency, not network --- the sub has no
independent value from the relationship. Our cross-tenant model creates
mutual value, which is the foundation of true network effects.

